[36m(TaskRunner pid=25208)[0m ### Step 1: Use the given condition $\overline{IA} \perp \overline{OI}$
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25208)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25208)[0m This is a key geometric condition. Let's recall some properties:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25208)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25208)[0m - The incenter $I$ is the intersection of angle bisectors.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25208)[0m - The circumcenter $O$ is the center of the circumscribed circle.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25208)[0m - The line $OI$ is the line joining the incenter and circumcenter.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25208)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25208)[0m We are told that $\overline{IA} \perp \overline{OI}$, which implies that the vector $\vec{IA}$ is perpendicular to the vector $\vec{OI}$.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25208)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=25208)[0m This is a very strong condition. Let's try to use it in a coordinate geometry approach.
